 "intro": "Outside Panormus (Palermo), Sicily, 250 BC. Hasdrubal has come with a large army and about a hundred elephants. Since the defeat in Africa five years ago, Roman troops have feared elephants. You are the proconsul Lucius Caecilius Metellus, inside the city.",
 "debrief": "In Polybius’s account, Metellus kept his army inside Panormus until Hasdrubal came close, then posted light troops with stacks of javelins along the ditch before the walls. The elephant drivers charged ahead of their army, the elephants were wounded by the missiles and stampeded back into their own troops, and Metellus sallied from a side gate into the disordered Carthaginians. He captured the elephants and paraded them in his triumph. The other choices are what-ifs.",
